# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /public/build IS COMMITTED. It used to be ignored, which is the Laravel
# default and the right default almost everywhere.
#
# It is wrong here. The server has no Node — it is shared hosting, and
# `npm run build` cannot run on it — so the compiled CSS and JS have to
# arrive some other way. Deploys are `git pull`. Anything not in the
# repository therefore does not arrive at all.
#
# What that cost: the stylesheet on dbelo.com stayed at whatever had once
# been uploaded by hand, while every deploy brought new Blade files with
# new Tailwind classes. Tailwind only emits a class that existed at build
# time, so those elements rendered with no CSS whatever — not broken,
# unpainted — and nothing failed anywhere. Admin → Diagnostics caught it
# a day later, which is a day of looking at a half-painted site.
#
# The cost of committing it, stated plainly: every deploy commit carries a
# ~360 KB stylesheet and a ~190 KB bundle, the repository grows, and a
# conflict in these files is meaningless noise — resolve one by rebuilding,
# never by merging. For one developer deploying with `git pull`, that is
# the cheaper of the two mistakes.
#
# THE RULE THAT COMES WITH IT: `npm run build` before the commit, every
# time a Blade file or a stylesheet changed. The build is now part of the
# deliverable, not a local side effect.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/public/fonts-manifest.dev.json
/public/hot
/public/storage
/storage/*.key
/storage/pail
/vendor
.env
.env.backup
.env.production
.phpactor.json
.phpunit.result.cache
Homestead.json
Homestead.yaml
